=== PROPOSAL ===
  ABSTAINED - no permitted action fits the evidence
  if wrong    : This abstention is wrong if a dispatch against Postgres or the broker shows the order table was populated by a prior workload and the new consumer is replaying from a stale offset — in which case the correct fix is restore_data (clear/reconcile the order table or advance the offset), which this executor cannot perform (restore_store is UNAVAILABLE, T6.2). It is also wrong if fresh metrics show the 23505 rate is already zero and the burst at 09:43:11–09:44:06 was self-limiting, in which case there is nothing to remediate at all. Note that the metrics here cannot settle this: the baseline window returned zero samples and three intervals had no traffic [tr_87ce6ad1d4f7], so neither onset nor current state is measurable, and an effect from any action would be largely unobservable.
  rests on    : tr_079b4ae39041, tr_3a2a5fdbff47, tr_2b3aecdb27d3, tr_d652adf5048e, tr_87ce6ad1d4f7
  execution   : not executed - a proposal is a claim, never the change (ADR-0028 §1)

context: 22913 pushed / 5256 pulled (pull rate 0.1866), 2 section(s) dropped

=== VERDICT ===
  fault class : datastore_corruption
  fix class   : restore_data
  confidence  : low
  evidence    : tr_d652adf5048e, tr_2b3aecdb27d3, tr_3a2a5fdbff47, tr_079b4ae39041, tr_87ce6ad1d4f7
  root cause  : Accounting's order consumer is failing to persist orders because the inserts it attempts duplicate rows that already exist: PostgreSQL rejects them with SQLSTATE 23505 on order_pkey. The database is reachable and healthy, the broker receive hop succeeds in milliseconds, and the errored span is a leaf inside accounting's own EF Core/Npgsql write path [tr_d652adf5048e, tr_2b3aecdb27d3, tr_3a2a5fdbff47]. The dense burst of ~20 identical violations in ~3 seconds followed by trailing events, each as a separate trace rather than an in-trace retry, is the signature of the same order messages being re-delivered and re-inserted against a store whose contents already hold those primary keys [tr_3a2a5fdbff47, tr_2b3aecdb27d3]. The only change in 24 hours is a first-time container creation ~3 minutes before onset by platform-automation, with no deploy, image bump, or config edit [tr_079b4ae39041] — consistent with a freshly created consumer replaying from an existing offset onto an already-populated order table. The mechanism is the state of accounting's store versus the messages being replayed, not an exhausted resource, a slow dependency, or a wrong configuration value.
  OPEN        : The conflicting primary-key values and Kafka consumer offsets are unrecoverable from the logs [tr_3a2a5fdbff47, tr_d652adf5048e], so whether the same orders are being re-delivered was never directly confirmed.
  OPEN        : Whether the order table was pre-populated (e.g. seeded or surviving from a prior workload) before the new container was created [tr_079b4ae39041] is unestablished.
  OPEN        : Metrics are too sparse to characterise onset: no baseline samples and gaps with zero traffic [tr_87ce6ad1d4f7], so the true start time and whether errors persist now are unknown.
  OPEN        : No dispatch examined the Postgres instance or the broker directly; both log results were truncated.

